      "marks": 5,
      "answer": "$\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\cot\\theta$.",
      "official_mark_scheme_text": "State or imply $dx/d\\theta=2\\sin2\\theta$ or $dy/d\\theta=2+2\\cos2\\theta$ — B1.\n\nUse $dy/dx=(dy/d\\theta)\\div(dx/d\\theta)$ — M1.\n\nObtain $dy/dx=(2+2\\cos2\\theta)/(2\\sin2\\theta)$ — A1, OE.\n\nUse correct double-angle formulae — M1.\n\nObtain $dy/dx=\\cot\\theta$ correctly — A1, AG. The numerator must be simplified in terms of $\\cos\\theta$.\n\nAlternative method 1: start by using both correct double-angle formulae, e.g. $x=3-(2\\cos^2\\theta-1)$ and $y=2\\theta+2\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta$ — M1; obtain $dx/d\\theta$ or $dy/d\\theta$ — B1; use $dy/dx=[2+2(\\cos^2\\theta-\\sin^2\\theta)]/(4\\cos\\theta\\sin\\theta)$ — M1 A1; simplify correctly to $dy/dx=\\cot\\theta$ — A1, AG.\n\nAlternative method 2: set $t=2\\theta$ and state $dx/dt=\\sin t$ or $dy/dt=1+\\cos t$ — B1; use $dy/dx=(dy/dt)\\div(dx/dt)$ — M1; obtain $(1+\\cos t)/\\sin t$ — A1, OE; use correct double-angle formulae — M1; obtain $dy/dx=\\cot\\theta$ — A1.\n\nTotal [5].",
